    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d470-a1">

                <pb facs="0885" n="781" sameAs="frus:frus1943CairoTehran/pg781"/>

                <head>Enclosure</head>
                <p>A schedule of operations for 1944 is set forth in the appendix.<anchor n="7"
                        xml:id="d470-f1"/> Target dates which have been determined after careful
                    consideration of prospective means and of time and space factors, are presented
                    for planning purposes only. We are convinced that the sequence of operations
                    must be flexible; we must be prepared to take all manner of short cuts made
                    possible by developments in the situation. The four primary developments which
                    may permit short cuts are: <list>
                        <label><hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>.</label>
                        <item>Early defeat of the Japanese
                            Fleet.</item>
                        <label><hi rendition="#italic">b</hi>.</label>
                        <item>Sudden withdrawal of Japanese forces from areas (as from
                            Kiska).</item>
                        <label><hi rendition="#italic">c</hi>.</label>
                        <item>Increase in our means such as by acceleration of the assault
                            shipbuilding program and by an earlier defeat of Germany than 1 October
                            1944.</item>
                        <label><hi rendition="#italic">d</hi>.</label>
                        <item>The early collaboration of the U. S. S. E. in the war against
                            Japan.</item>
                    </list></p>
                <p>We have directed that further study be conducted and plans made and kept up to
                    date for the conditions assumed in <hi rendition="#italic">c</hi> and <hi
                        rendition="#italic">d</hi>.</p>
                <p>We have directed that special attention be given to the optimum employment of the
                    enormous air forces which will be released upon the defeat of Germany.</p>
                <p>We have directed that a study be made for the optimum use, timing, and deployment
                    in the war against Japan of very long range bombers.</p>
            </div>
